Keying simulations
==================

Runs of the iambic keyer, one plot each. Seven sets are kept here, three
of them at 30 WPM and four at 40, and paddle_sweep.py can produce more
at any speed.

Each plot has three panels against one time axis, zeroed on the paddle
that closes first:

  - what the operator did: the two paddles' closures
  - what the operator heard: the sidetone as rendered, each tone named
    and carrying the length it actually came out at
  - what the K4 was sent: every KZD and KZU, key down above the line
    and key up below

Every tone comes out at exactly the length its KZU carries -- 40ms or
120ms at 30 WPM, 30ms or 90ms at 40 -- across every scenario and every
run of them. The sidetone catch-up shortens tones a fraction where the
audio has drifted past the lag the keyer holds, and over the device
these were run against it never has: the delay sits at what was
measured of it. A device that drifts would show the tones a shade
under, and the K4 still told the full length, so nothing on the air is
ever short.


keying_30wpm_swap_*.png -- twenty-one runs
------------------------------------------

The dit paddle closes at time zero, and at the moment named in the
file's name the operator releases the dit paddle and closes the dah,
holding it. That moment moves 4ms per run, from 84ms to 164ms.

What they are here to show is that once the first dah has gone out, no
dit follows it. Across the sweep the only thing that varies is how many
dits precede the switch, which is what should vary:

     84-104ms   dit dah dah dah
    108-164ms   dit dit dah dah dah

Before the dit up memory was made to require that the paddle had also
been closed during the element it is remembered from, 13 of these 21
runs put an unasked-for dit after the first dah -- as element 3 below
108ms and as element 4 from 140ms up.

Summary: summary_30wpm_swap.json


keying_30wpm_dit-tap_*.png -- fifty-six runs
--------------------------------------------

The mirror of the dah tap below: the dah paddle closes at time zero and
is held for the whole run, and the dit paddle is tapped for 5ms at the
moment named in the file's name. That moment moves 4ms per run, from
80ms to 300ms -- nearly five dah element periods, a dah being three
times a dit.

Every run inserts exactly one dit into the stream of dahs, never two
and never none, and the dahs resume after it. What varies is only which
element the dit becomes:

     80-184ms   dah dit dah dah dah dah
    188-300ms   dah dah dit dah dah dah

The boundary sits at 188ms, not at the 160ms where the second dah
begins. The 28ms between the two is send_burst asking the paddles a
second time: an element is started before it is known what it is, and
read again once its sidetone can be heard, so a tap arriving up to
184ms still catches the element that began at 160ms. That second
reading is what the band either side of a boundary is made of.

Summary: summary_30wpm_dit-tap.json


keying_30wpm_squeeze_*.png -- a hundred and twenty-five runs
------------------------------------------------------------

Both paddles closed at time zero and both let go together at the moment
named in the file's name, moving 4ms per run from 4ms to 500ms. The
squeeze alternates on a 240ms cycle at this speed, so that is two whole
cycles and a little over: the release falls in every part of one, and
the pattern is then seen to repeat rather than assumed to.

    release       released during   elements sent
      4-104ms     the first dit     dit dah
    108-264ms     the first dah     dit dah dit
    268-344ms     the second dit    dit dah dit dah
    348-500ms     the second dah    dit dah dit dah dit

Each band ends exactly where the next tone becomes audible: the second
at 104ms, the third at 264, the fourth at 344. That is the rule below
working as written, and it is why the sweep is cut from the sidetone
and not from the keyed grid.

The 4ms step lands on all three of those instants, so in three runs the
squeeze is let go of at the very moment a tone begins -- a moment the
rule has nothing to say about, the space before it having just run out
and the tone after it not yet having sounded for any length of time at
all. Those three are allowed either answer, and the band they are shown
in above is the one the keyer chose. See squeeze_verdict.

This is the case iambic B exists for. The rule, stated against what the
operator can hear: a squeeze let go of while an element's sidetone is
sounding, or during the space after it, owes exactly one more element --
the opposite of that one -- and nothing after it.

Alone among the sets here this one is checked as well as drawn. The
heard timeline is cut at each tone's start, so a window is a tone plus
the space following it and every release falls in one, bar the three
that land on a cut. Each run says which element it was released during,
what was owed for it, and what actually followed, and the run ends with
a count. All of them follow the rule.

Nothing about the check is tied to 30 WPM. There is a second set of it
below at 40, and run besides at 20, 25, 35, 45 and 50 the keyer follows
the rule in every run of every one.

Getting all of them took three things, and the sweep is what said each
of them was needed. It no longer speaks to the second at all, that
having since been made good another way; the paragraph is kept for what
it says about the first.

The memories measure a paddle against the previous element, and they
measure it from when that element was heard, not from when it was
keyed. The hand works to the sidetone: an element is not there to be
answered, nor to be still squeezing through, until it can be heard.
Measured from the keyed start instead, 21 of the 125 go wrong -- the
last handful of every band, 84 to 100ms and again at 244 to 260, 324 to
340 and 480 to 500. Those are the releases falling after the next
element was keyed but before any of it was audible, and each puts an
unasked-for element after it.

That heard instant is where the output callback really placed this
element's edge. Where an edge is never placed at all -- the stream
replaced under it and its queue dropped -- the keyer falls back on the
keyed start plus the delay the last edge it did place was given, and
over this device the two come to the same instant: made to use that
estimate throughout, this set comes out unchanged, every run of it. So
it says nothing either way about taking the placed edge over the
estimate. It said more when the estimate was reckoned from a lag saved
between sessions rather than from what the audio had just done.

A burst's first element is the exception, and is measured from its
keyed start. That element the operator began themselves, by closing the
paddle; it is the only one whose beginning is known to them without
hearing it, so a squeeze let go of before it can be heard has still
been let go of during it. Measuring it like the rest loses all six
releases inside the lag, from 4 to 24ms.

None of this is visible from the keyed grid. previous_elt_ts is the
instant the element was keyed -- send_burst used to read the clock at
the top of its loop, a whole inter-element gap earlier for every
element but a burst's first, which the lag happened to paper over.

The set is here because 6c715bb broke exactly this and no sweep noticed.
That commit required a remembered paddle to have been closed during the
element it is remembered from, and a released squeeze is the opposite --
closed from before the element began and let go part way through it --
so none of them got its element until efd5c5c. Restoring the broken
guard today leaves the swap, tap and dit-tap sweeps byte identical, and
takes this one from 125 to 25. So does every other way of breaking it
that has been tried: nothing but this sweep notices any of them.

Summary: summary_30wpm_squeeze.json


keying_40wpm_squeeze_*.png -- ninety-four runs
-----------------------------------------------

The same squeeze at 40 WPM, where a dit is 30ms and a dah 90. The cycle
is 180ms rather than 240, so the sweep runs from 4ms to 376ms to cover
the same two cycles and a little over -- three quarters of the ground
in three quarters of the time, at the same 4ms step.

    release       released during   elements sent
      4-80ms      the first dit     dit dah
     84-200ms     the first dah     dit dah dit
    204-264ms     the second dit    dit dah dit dah
    268-376ms     the second dah    dit dah dit dah dit

All ninety-four follow the rule.

It is here because nothing was watching above 30 WPM, and the speed is
not incidental to this keyer. Two of the things the sets here exist to
hold in place are measured against an element's length, and a dit at 40
WPM is a quarter shorter than at 30. A dit's key up is the tightest
edge in the scheme -- it goes into the queue only once the tone can be
heard, so what is left of the dit after the audio's delay is all the
notice it gets -- and it was that margin, not anything visible at 30
WPM, that sidetone_up_edge_margin_seconds was added for.

The tones start at 24, 84, 204, 264 and 384ms, and the 4ms step lands
on three of them. Those three are the boundary runs described above,
and at this speed they do not all fall the same way: 84 and 204 are
counted as during the tone beginning, 264 as during the one ending.
Which way a run comes down is rounding rather than keying, which is why
squeeze_verdict allows either. Before it did, the release at 204ms was
reported as a failure and was not one.

Summary: summary_40wpm_squeeze.json


keying_40wpm_swap_*.png -- sixteen runs
----------------------------------------

The swap at 40 WPM, from 64ms to 124ms. The same two bands as at 30 WPM
and nothing else in them, which is what the set is here to say:

     64-76ms    dit dah dah dah
     80-124ms   dit dit dah dah dah

Summary: summary_40wpm_swap.json


keying_40wpm_dit-tap_*.png -- forty-two runs
---------------------------------------------

The dah held and the dit tapped for 5ms, from 60ms to 224ms.

     60-136ms   dah dit dah dah dah dah
    140-224ms   dah dah dit dah dah dah

Exactly one dit in every run, as at 30 WPM. The boundary is at 140ms
where the second dah is keyed at 120 and heard at 144: what decides a
tap is the last look at the paddles, and a dit's key up leaves so little
of a dit to be queued in at this speed that the look no longer waits for
the whole of the tone.

That is the band this set was added for. Between the key up being given
its margin and a tap being measured from the last look rather than from
the heard start, three runs of these forty-two and the sixteen below
lost the tapped element altogether -- the operator tapped a paddle and
nothing came out. Nothing at 30 WPM showed it.

Summary: summary_40wpm_dit-tap.json


keying_40wpm_tap_*.png -- sixteen runs
---------------------------------------

The mirror: the dit held throughout and the dah tapped for 5ms, from
64ms to 124ms.

     64-76ms    dit dah dit dit
     80-124ms   dit dit dah dit

A tap is a paddle closed and released inside one element, which is what
the memory exists for, so this is the set the memory guard is answerable
to. At 30 WPM it is run rather than kept, there being nothing in it to
look at: the guard leaves that sweep untouched, all 21 runs, every
element and every KZD and KZU identical to what they were before it.

Summary: summary_40wpm_tap.json


Running them
------------

    python3 paddle_sweep.py <a copy of k4companion named *.py> <outdir> \
        <tap|dit-tap|swap|squeeze> [wpm]

The speed defaults to 30. At any other, the swept moment and the length
of the run are scaled by it rather than left where they were, so that a
sweep covers the same ground at every speed: the ranges above are cut
to elements, not to milliseconds. The step stays at 4ms.

The harness drives the real Keyer over a virtual clock: paddle events
go in through send_paddle_event on the thread a paddle's device would
report on, send_iambic runs on the keyer's thread a hop later, and the
sidetone edges plotted are the frames next_key_edge actually placed.
Nothing about the keyer is reimplemented.

It takes a copy of the program with a .py suffix because it imports it
as a module.
